I'm getting a Rust borrow checker error: "cannot borrow `data` as mutable more than once at a time". I don't understand why this simple code fails:

let mut data = vec![1, 2, 3];
let slice1 = &data;
let slice2 = &mut data;
slice2.push(4);

final_entries.response:
This is a fundamental safety feature of Rust. You currently have an immutable reference (`slice1`) to `data`, which means anyone could be reading the data at any time. If Rust allowed a mutable reference (`slice2`) simultaneously, you could modify `data` while `slice1` is still using it, creating a data race or dangling reference.

The fix depends on what you actually need:

**If you only need `slice1` temporarily:**
```rust
let mut data = vec![1, 2, 3];
{
    let slice1 = &data;
    println!("First element: {}", slice1[0]);
} // slice1 goes out of scope here
let slice2 = &mut data;
slice2.push(4);
```

**If you don't actually need both references at the same time, reorder your code** so the immutable borrow ends before you start the mutable one.

**If you genuinely need independent access**, consider cloning:
```rust
let slice1 = data.clone();
let slice2 = &mut data;
slice2.push(4);
```

Tell me what `slice1` is used for and I can suggest the best approach for your case.
